WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.320
The Christmas pudding is in decline.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.600
In a recent survey,
less than half the population

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.080
said they included one
on their yuletide table.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.720
And that figure hides
a significant age bias.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.400
The younger you are,
the less you fancy a bit of pudding.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.240
How has this dish,
once emblematic of Britishness,

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.040
come to such a sorry state?

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.360
What we call Christmas pudding has
its roots in the medieval period,

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.360
when a rich concoction
called plum pottage

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.320
became associated
with winter feasting.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.760
Pottage was just a name
for a soupy stew,

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.680
and this one contained
beef, spice and dried fruit.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:30.960
When a pudding version appeared,

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.000
sometime around
the end of the Tudor era,

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.600
the two co-existed happily
for many decades.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.640
Plum pudding was similar in style,
but with no actual meat,

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.040
just lots of beef suet,

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.560
and was thickened with breadcrumbs
and flour with added eggs,

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:43.760
until the mixture was thick enough

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.480
to be put in a wetted,
floured cloth and boiled.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.320
The resulting speckled brown,
cannonball-shaped pudding

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.000
wasn’t sophisticated
but was delicious.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.080
It tumbled rapidly into recipe books

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.440
with as many variants
as there were writers.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.560
It was part of the main meal,

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.400
sometimes eaten before the meats
but more often with them.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.480
And it quickly became seen as the
ideal accompaniment to roast beef,

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:04.880
a bit like modern chutney.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.040
Beef and plum pudding became the
English dishes of the 18th Century.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.600
The plum pudding was eaten
widely across the winter,

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.120
though it was hardly
a dish for the poor

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.640
with all its spice
and expensive imported fruit.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:16.760
It could be made in advance

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.720
though it was more often
prepared when needed,

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.560
and it would keep for months
making it ideal as a gift.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.760
However, as the 19th Century wore on

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.880
it became increasingly
associated with feasting,

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:27.600
in particular at Christmas

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:28.800
because it was eaten with beef

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.120
and beef was so ubiquitous
on the Christmas table,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.880
the plum pudding
itself came to be seen

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:34.920
as a particularly Christmassy food.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.360
And was even served
when no beef was in the offing.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.160
In 1845, Eliza Acton
became the first person

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.800
to publish a recipe for it
called Christmas pudding.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.000
And it slowly slunk
to later in the meal.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.520
It also got gradually sweeter
and squishier.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.120
For it was now often moulded
in an ornate copper mould

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:51.440
for the tables of the rich,

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.760
or a simple bowl
for those of lesser means.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.400
Habits change and the 20th Century
left the plum pudding behind,

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.360
though as Christmas pudding
it survived.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.320
No longer eaten as part of the main
meal, it was now a sweet.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.640
And served increasingly
with accompaniments deemed fitting

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:05.960
for its new definition -

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.000
cream or custard or brandy butter.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.920
The tradition of setting it on fire
was a Victorian one,

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:11.920
but in the 1920s

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.280
it garnered more mythology
such as the taking, rather seriously,

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.640
of the old Victorian joke

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:19.160
that the pudding
should be made on stir up Sunday -

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:20.880
the fifth Sunday before Christmas.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:23.680
A sixpence was sometimes
added to the mixture as well,

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.680
a pale shadow of
another festive tradition,

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.400
this time related to the ancestor
of the Christmas cake -

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:29.440
Twelfth cake -

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:33.320
which had had tokens concealed in it
to be found amongst much revelry.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.840
Today our Christmas dinner
has swollen to such proportions

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:37.360
that a hefty pudding
with a rich sauce

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.400
can sometimes seem
too much to handle.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.880
Perhaps this year
we should go back to basics -

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.560
beef with gravy and a slice
of plum pudding, anyone?

